{"id":28754634479482,"type":"song","name":"Brown-Eyed Handsome Man","artist":"Chuck Berry","year":1959,"weight":13,"info":{"img":null},"artist_ref":{"id":61849751677173,"name":"Chuck Berry","info":{"img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Chuck_Berry_%288275961%29.jpeg?width=500"}},"labels":[],"genres":[],"lists":[],"podcast":[{"kind":"podcast","title":null,"body":"Despite being considered a classic, this Chuck Berry song did not register on the pop charts, highlighting the inconsistency in his early chart performance. \"brown-eyed handsome man... had totally failed to hit the pop charts at all.\"","year":1959,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"A History of Rock Music in 500 Songs","episode":"Episode 67: \"Johnny B. Goode\", by Chuck Berry","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sHlA-qihuNw"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":null,"body":"This Chuck Berry song was greatly admired by Elvis Presley and Jerry Lee Lewis during the Million Dollar Quartet jam session, where they made several attempts to play it. \"They're all hugely impressed with Chuck Berry's brown-eyed handsome man and have settled goes at playing it.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"A History of Rock Music in 500 Songs","episode":"Episode 51: \"Matchbox\" by Carl Perkins","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0Nrjr0AGd3U"}]}],"book_tracks":[],"others":[{"id":243827202956491,"type":"song","name":"Sweet Little Sixteen","year":1958,"weight":28},{"id":130532461407305,"type":"song","name":"Johnny B. Goode","year":1958,"weight":24},{"id":85965292584055,"type":"song","name":"My Ding-a-Ling","year":1972,"weight":20},{"id":2456478370569,"type":"song","name":"Rock and Roll Music","year":1957,"weight":19},{"id":279254257664730,"type":"song","name":"You Can't Catch Me","year":1959,"weight":16},{"id":2202523180344,"type":"work","name":"Roll Over Beethoven","year":1956,"weight":16},{"id":115579487984046,"type":"song","name":"Too Much Monkey Business","year":1959,"weight":13},{"id":1282407604